    "q-74091": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74091",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot k+0\\cdot 1+k\\cdot 5+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-2k+0+5k-6 \\\\  & =3k-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k-6 & =0 \\\\ 3k & =6 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
